#################### 1.Recently handled issue in Playwright framework
S (Situation)
Recently, while running our Playwright regression suite in the CI pipeline, I noticed that a few test cases were showing as passed, but when I manually verified the tables in database, some of the fields are not updated.
T (Task)
My responsibility was to identify why the automation was passing incorrectly and improve the framework so that such issues would be detected automatically in future runs.
A (Action)
I analysed the Playwright trace reports and noticed that during the test run, the UI flow continued without throwing any assertion failures, but the application had a hidden failure.
 - Some of the values entered were not actually committed/bound to the input field.
 - Then as part of further analysis, when I retried manually, I found similar issue. When I checked console log, I coud find that the application was throwing an uncaughtJavaScript exception.
Since our test scripts were only validating UI elements and not monitoring JavaScript errors, Playwright did not fail the test.
To fix this,
I enhanced the framework by adding global listeners in the base setup:
I captured page errors using page.on("pageerror")
I stored all JS errors during execution in an array.
Finally, 
I added a validation in afterEach() hook so that if any JavaScript exception occurred, the test would fail immediately with a clear error message.
At the end of every test, afterEach() checks whether any JavaScript errors were recorded. If yes, the framework fails the test immediately and prints the exact error message in the report.
This ensured that even if UI validations passed, the test would fail if the application had internal JavaScript crashes.
R (Result)
After implementing this framework enhancement:
Those tests started failing correctly in CI instead of giving false positives
We were able to raise defects earlier to developers
The issue was fixed in the application side
And overall, the stability and reliability of our Playwright regression suite improved significantly, especially for critical card payment flows where session expiry issues are common.
🔥 Strong Closing Line (Optional)
So this improvement helped ensure our automation suite validates not only UI behavior but also application stability by catching hidden JavaScript failures.




#################### 2. Why Playwright?
I prefer Playwright because it is a modern automation framework that comes with many features out-of-the-box, which makes test automation faster, stable, and easier to maintain compared to Selenium.

Key reasons:
✅ Auto-wait and Smart Synchronization
Playwright has an inbuilt auto-wait mechanism, meaning it automatically waits for elements to be visible, enabled, and ready before interacting. This reduces synchronization issues and makes tests less flaky.
✅ Built-in Test Runner & Reporting
Unlike Selenium, Playwright comes with its own test runner, detailed HTML reports, and supports annotations, tagging, and filtering tests easily.
✅ Parallel Execution Support
Playwright supports parallel test execution by default, which helps reduce execution time significantly, especially for large regression suites.
✅ Powerful Debugging Features
It provides advanced debugging options like:
Trace Viewer
Video recording
Screenshots
Snapshots
Retry mechanism
This makes defect investigation and root cause analysis much easier.
✅ Supports UI + API Testing Together
Playwright supports API testing using request fixtures, so we can validate backend APIs and UI flows in the same framework. This is very useful for end-to-end testing.
✅ Cross-Browser and Cross-Platform Support
Playwright supports Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit, which helps validate applications across different browsers consistently.
✅ Built-in Smart Locators
Playwright supports multiple locator strategies like:
getByRole() (best for accessibility + stability)
getByText()
getByLabel()
getByPlaceholder()
getByTestId()
These are more readable and closer to how real users interact with the UI.

